      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© American Academy of Otolaryngology\u2014Head and Neck Surgery Foundation 2015.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To investigate the role of obesity/overweight on the prognosis of sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSHL)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "Retrospective cohort study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Outpatient department of a community hospital."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS AND METHODS",
            "content": "We collected 254 adult patients with SSHL from a community hospital. The odd ratios of body mass index (BMI) or obesity/overweight (BMI ≥25 kg/m(2)) on the recovery of SSHL were evaluated with multivariate logistic regression analysis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "There were 120 (47.2%) patients in the nonobesity group (BMI <25 kg/m(2)) and 134 (52.8%) patients in the obesity/overweight group (BMI ≥25 kg/m(2)). The complete and partial recovery rates were 10.0% and 49.2% in the nonobesity group and 9.7% and 47.0% in the obesity/overweight group, respectively. Univariate logistic regression showed that BMI had no significant association with recovery of SSHL (odds ratio [OR] of complete and partial recovery versus no recovery = 1.04, 95% confidence interval [CI] = 0.965-1.113, P = .327). Multivariate logistic regression analysis also showed that BMI (OR = 1.04, 95% CI = 0.964-1.131, P = .292) was not significantly associated with the recovery of SSHL for all subjects, after adjusting for all considered variables. Also, obesity/overweight (BMI ≥25 kg/m(2)) had no significant association with the recovery of SSHL."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Obesity/overweight would appear to have no significant effect on the prognosis of SSHL."
          }
        ]
      },
